P('Correspondence: Isarar Siddique, '+A['corresponding_email']+'. ORCID: 0009-0005-2305-9579.')
H('Abstract')
P(f'''Collection conditions and biological exposure can produce overlapping brain transcriptomic responses. We reanalysed seven NASA deposits comprising 129 libraries and 83 animals using animal-restricted inference, count models, paired regional tests and ground-derived expression transformations. Unflown collection cohorts differed in rapid-response genes (mean log2 effect {pv('RRRM2_pooled_GC_ISST_vs_LAR','rPRG'):.3f}, q={pv('RRRM2_pooled_GC_ISST_vs_LAR','rPRG','q_BH_all_tests_in_family'):.3f}). Count-model directions agreed in {int(nb.same_effect_direction.sum())}/{len(nb)} panel comparisons; no paired panel or cross-cohort gene test survived its respective correction. In 362 overlapping ground holdouts with training-only preprocessing, sensor discrimination was perfect in both RRRM-2 regions and mean AUC 0.964 in RR-23. Yet original score loadings worsened outside-sensor prediction by 9.1% in cerebellum and 3.5% in RR-23 relative to the nuisance-only baseline. Ground-calibrated score subtraction was an oblique projection with real-data gain 8.13–15.31. A closest-loading constraint capped gain while retaining sensor-score removal. Across 2,600 independent simulated datasets, bounding reduced amplification under changed coupling but could leave substantial nuisance. Joint animal bootstraps exposed attenuation uncertainty. The real-data dissociation between reproducible collection scores and genome-wide loading transfer supports explicit stability constraints and collection-controlled validation, rather than equating removed expression energy with recovered biology.''')
P('Keywords: spaceflight; brain transcriptomics; collection confounding; oblique projection; paired analysis; reproducibility; negative-binomial models.')
H('Introduction')
P('Spaceflight combines altered gravity, radiation, constrained housing, handling and return-related stress with differences in sample collection and preservation. Transcriptomic studies provide unusually rich access to these responses, but their interpretation depends on whether experimental contrasts distinguish the exposure of interest from the way tissue was obtained. Reviews of spaceflight biology identify oxidative stress, mitochondrial regulation, immune responses and neuronal adaptation as recurring themes [1]. NASA\'s Open Science Data Repository (OSDR) and consensus RNA-seq processing make comparative analyses possible [2,3]. Standardized computational processing, however, cannot by itself make distinct collection cohorts exchangeable or turn multiple tissues from one animal into independent exposure replicates.')
P('Brain tissue is particularly vulnerable to an ambiguity between in vivo regulation and changes associated with collection. Neuronal activity can induce distinct rapid and secondary gene-expression programs [6,7], while tissue processing can generate artifactual or confounding glial signatures [5]. Sample collection during spaceflight introduces additional logistical constraints that affect the suitability of material for single-cell and spatial assays [14]. These observations motivate response-panel diagnostics, but they do not justify assigning a molecular time since euthanasia from a cross-sectional bulk RNA-seq profile. Nor does a reduction in glial or endothelial transcript abundance directly measure neuroinflammation or blood–brain barrier permeability.')
P('Previous mouse studies reported spaceflight-associated expression changes and oxidative injury in brain tissue [15,16]. Spatial and single-nucleus multiomics in RR-3 localized responses to regions and cell populations, and showed limited concordance with bulk expression from the same mice [17]. That comparison is valuable biological context, but shared animals do not provide independent confirmation of an expression-correction algorithm. Differences among strains, sex, mission duration, handling, anatomical material and normalization also complicate a universal cross-mission signature. Bulk panels can reveal consistent associations while remaining unable to separate cell abundance from within-cell regulation.')
P('Recent small-RNA analysis across 686 mouse samples from 13 organs reported predominantly age-independent spaceflight responses [21]. A protein-level RR-1 study reported decreased brain inflammasome activation, with no significant flight–ground differences in the assayed cytokines [22]. These findings further caution against assigning one inflammatory direction across missions or equating bulk transcriptional markers with functional immune activation. Neither study validates the ground-derived correction evaluated here.')
P('The statistical difficulty is broader than spaceflight. Unwanted variation can dominate high-dimensional expression, and adjustments that preserve a chosen group contrast can exaggerate downstream confidence [4,18]. Surrogate-variable and control-gene methods illustrate how auxiliary information can help model nuisance structure [11,12,19]. Their success depends on what the auxiliary variation represents and whether its relationship to target expression transfers. A ground-associated response is not automatically a valid negative control for flight biology. When biology and nuisance share a response direction, subtraction can remove either or both.')
P('We therefore framed the study around identifiability and stability rather than maximizing the number of significant genes. We audited sample identities across seven bulk brain deposits, re-estimated collection and exposure contrasts with animal-level inference, and tested regional interactions using genuinely paired cerebellum–hippocampus animals. We fitted negative-binomial models directly to integer counts as a separate estimator check and screened for directionally reproducible regional effects across cohorts. Finally, we analysed the geometry of two ground-derived transformations, constructed a gain-bounded score operator, and challenged all methods with known-truth and high-gain simulations. The mathematical construction uses standard projection and constrained least-squares geometry; the contribution is its explicit application, audit and failure analysis in these brain datasets. This study tests whether a correction can be numerically controlled without confusing sensor removal with biological recovery.')
H('Results')
H('Sample identity defines the available evidence',2)
P('The seven count matrices contained 129 libraries. Summing technical libraries within Source Name animal and deposit produced 126 animal–region columns and 83 distinct animals across RRRM-2, RR-10 and RR-23 (Table 1). OSD-561 and OSD-562 contained 26 and 27 libraries, respectively, representing 29 animals in total rather than 53 independent animals (Fig. 1). Twenty-four animals contributed both cerebellum and hippocampus. OSD-563 and OSD-564 provided ten additional matched pairs from RR-10, whose left-hemisphere deposit OSD-612 contained nine of that cohort\'s animals. OSD-613 contained 18 other RRRM-2 animals. Anatomical deposits thus represent additional observations and comparison contexts, not seven independent missions.')
P('RR-23 cerebellum data contained 29 libraries from 26 animals: nine flight, nine habitat ground controls (HGC) and eight vivarium ground controls (VGC). Three technical replicates were retained by count summation. The Source Name identities agreed with deposited RFID mappings across regions. Independently retrieved NASA STAR count files for the two core deposits matched the bundled gene identifiers, sample identifiers and every numerical count. This verification establishes the origin of the processed inputs; it does not repeat raw-read alignment or establish the correctness of every deposited experimental field.')
P('Seven location entries from six core animals disagreed with the collection cohort inferred from Source Name. We retained these observations in the primary analysis and added exclusion sensitivities that removed every region of each affected animal. Source Name denotes a cohort designation; ground controls assigned to the ISS-terminal cohort were ground animals. Exclusion changed age support, so its loss of significance cannot be interpreted as proof that the full effect was caused by erroneous records. The conflicts remain a specific limitation of the deposited metadata, with both representations exported for review.')
P('Count-derived diagnostics and principal-component analyses include every animal–region observation from all seven deposits (Supplementary Fig. 2). Condition-associated multivariate partial R² varied from 0.073 to 0.265. These descriptive differences confirm substantial heterogeneity in expression structure; they do not measure a causal exposure fraction. Deposited RNA-integrity numbers were unavailable, and mitochondrial gene-count fractions are reported as composition measures rather than replacements for sequence-level quality or RNA integrity.')
