H('Ground collection cohorts share response programs with flight contrasts',2)
cg='RRRM2_pooled_GC_ISST_vs_LAR';cf='RRRM2_pooled_FLT_ISST_vs_LAR';hip='OSD562_hippocampus_FLT_ISST_vs_LAR'
P(f'''Pooled ground ISS-terminal (ISS-T) minus live-animal-return (LAR) comparisons showed a rapid primary-response panel effect of {pv(cg,'rPRG'):.3f} log2 (95% animal-bootstrap interval {pv(cg,'rPRG','bootstrap_lower95'):.3f}–{pv(cg,'rPRG','bootstrap_upper95'):.3f}; exact permutation p={pv(cg,'rPRG','p_animal_permutation'):.6f}; BH q={pv(cg,'rPRG','q_BH_all_tests_in_family'):.3f}). Ground secondary-response gene (SRG) effects were small ({pv(cg,'SRG'):.3f}, q={pv(cg,'SRG','q_BH_all_tests_in_family'):.3f}). The rapid-minus-secondary panel gradient was {val(g,'gradient_results.csv',dict(contrast=cg,sensitivity='design_cohort'),'rPRG_minus_SRG_mean_log2_effect'):.3f} log2 (p={val(g,'gradient_results.csv',dict(contrast=cg,sensitivity='design_cohort'),'p_animal_permutation'):.6f}; q={val(g,'gradient_results.csv',dict(contrast=cg,sensitivity='design_cohort'),'q_BH_all_tests_in_family'):.3f} across 29 gradient tests). This is evidence of collection-cohort-associated expression in unflown animals, not a clock or a causal estimate of a collection artifact.''')
P(f'''Pooled flight ISS-T minus LAR comparisons also showed rapid-response ({pv(cf,'rPRG'):.3f}, q={pv(cf,'rPRG','q_BH_all_tests_in_family'):.3f}) and heat-shock ({pv(cf,'HSP'):.3f}, q={pv(cf,'HSP','q_BH_all_tests_in_family'):.3f}) effects. In hippocampus, the flight cohort SRG effect was {pv(hip,'SRG'):.3f} log2 (q={pv(hip,'SRG','q_BH_all_tests_in_family'):.3f}), contradicting a uniform account in which secondary-response expression remains unchanged. These comparisons combine flight duration, Earth recovery and collection context: ISS-T animals spent 55–58 days on orbit, while LAR animals returned after 32 days and recovered for 24 days. Their difference cannot identify a single kinetic mechanism.''')
P('All 17 primary design contrasts and 12 metadata sensitivities entered one 232-test panel family; the gradient analyses formed a separate 29-test family (Fig. 2; Supplementary Tables). Eight inherited exploratory panels were retained, including the overlapping Sensor31 union. Panel overlap means these tests are not independent. Complete enumeration of whole-animal labels respected the stated age blocks and preserved all regional observations of an animal together. Small p values are conditional on exchangeability within those blocks and do not eliminate unmeasured collection confounding. No primary glia/complement panel comparison supported a multiplicity-adjusted universal inflammatory pattern.')
H('Count models support estimator agreement, with a qualified hippocampal candidate',2)
P(f'''Seven PyDESeq2 fits on collapsed integer counts yielded 15 regional contrasts and 120 panel comparisons. Panel mean-effect directions agreed in {int(nb.same_effect_direction.sum())}/{len(nb)} cases; the Pearson correlation of panel mean effects was {nb.mean_log2_effect.corr(nb.mean_NB_log2_effect):.3f} (Fig. 5a). This agreement indicates that the principal response associations were not solely consequences of the log-expression linear estimator. It is not a second biological sample or validation of an expression transform. Cook-filtered gene probabilities, unshrunk coefficients, dispersions and model configurations are retained, including unavailable probabilities.''')
hc='OSD562_hippocampus_FLT_vs_GC_within_ISS_T';ex='exclude_all_regions_of_conflicting_animals'
P(f'''Within hippocampal ISS-T animals, the flight-minus-ground SRG panel effect was {pv(hc,'SRG'):.3f} log2 (p={pv(hc,'SRG','p_animal_permutation'):.6f}; q={pv(hc,'SRG','q_BH_all_tests_in_family'):.3f}). After removing metadata-conflict animals, the estimate remained positive ({pv(hc,'SRG',s=ex):.3f}), but q increased to {pv(hc,'SRG','q_BH_all_tests_in_family',s=ex):.3f}. The full circadian effect was {pv(hc,'Circadian'):.3f} log2 (q={pv(hc,'Circadian','q_BH_all_tests_in_family'):.3f}); the exclusion estimate was {pv(hc,'Circadian',s=ex):.3f} (q={pv(hc,'Circadian','q_BH_all_tests_in_family',s=ex):.3f}). Fig. 5b displays the complete-family probabilities and intervals. These candidates justify collection-controlled follow-up; they do not establish a robust hippocampus-specific flight mechanism.''')
H('A direct flight by collection interaction identifies a qualified circadian candidate',2)
P(f'''An additional exploratory analysis tested the difference in flight-minus-ground effects between ISS-T and LAR directly, adjusting for age, within each core region (Supplementary Fig. 3). The hippocampal Circadian panel interaction was {iv('interaction_log2'):.3f} log2 (95% stratified animal-bootstrap interval {iv('bootstrap_lower95'):.3f} to {iv('bootstrap_upper95'):.3f}; restricted-model wild p={iv('p_wild_approximate'):.6f}; BH q={iv('q_BH_complete_panel_family'):.6f} across the complete, separately declared 32-test interaction family). With 499,999 wild draws, the Monte Carlo interval for the exceedance probability was {iv('p_Monte_Carlo_lower95'):.6f}–{iv('p_Monte_Carlo_upper95'):.6f}. The count-model panel mean agreed in direction ({iv('mean_NB_interaction_log2'):.3f}). This is a direct interaction estimate, rather than a comparison of significance in two strata.''')
P(f'''Every one-animal and one-gene omission retained a negative full-data hippocampal Circadian interaction, ranging from −0.459 to −0.365 and −0.452 to −0.360 log2, respectively. Metadata exclusions also retained the negative coefficient ({iv('interaction_log2','exclude_any_region_metadata_conflict'):.3f}), but corrected support was lost (q={iv('q_BH_complete_panel_family','exclude_any_region_metadata_conflict'):.3f}). Only one older FLT ISS-T animal remained in hippocampus and none in cerebellum. The additive-age model cannot identify age-dependent interactions in an empty cell. Thus the candidate has effect-direction and estimator support, with an important limitation in metadata and design support; it is not evidence of a causal flight clock.''')
P('Four additional integer-count interaction fits yielded 72,023 evaluable gene tests across both regions and metadata sensitivities, corrected as one complete exploratory family. Primary fits identified 109 cerebellar and seven hippocampal genes; 90 and two, respectively, also retained global q≤0.05 after exclusion. Cerebellar Hbb-bt and Hbb-bs interactions were −3.495 and −3.009 log2 (global q=0.000197 and 0.001406) and remained supported after exclusion. All seven available fixed erythroid-associated marker point estimates were negative in each primary region, although most individual markers did not pass the original global family. This pattern motivates a possible composition contribution (Supplementary Fig. 4); it neither proves blood contamination nor identifies a cell source or blood fraction. Target-omitted marker correlations after full-design adjustment were supplied to avoid simple self-correlation. These within-cohort interaction candidates are distinct from the cross-cohort reproducibility screen below.')
H('Pairing and reproducibility tests constrain biological claims',2)
P('We subtracted each paired animal\'s cerebellar panel expression from its hippocampal expression, then compared these regional differences across exposure and collection cohorts (Fig. 8). This directly tests a regional interaction instead of inferring specificity from significance in one region and nonsignificance in another. None of 104 paired panel tests passed BH q≤0.05; the minimum q was 0.198. The strongest nominal SRG regional ISS-T-minus-LAR effect in flight animals was +0.758 log2 (p=0.001905; q=0.198). Regional flight effects for SRG were −0.203 in ISS-T, −0.018 in LAR and −0.246 in RR-10, all q≥0.906. The approximate region-by-flight-by-collection interaction was −0.223, with restricted-model wild-bootstrap p=0.531 and q=0.906.')
P('Metadata exclusion removed all four older FLT ISS-T paired animals. Its ISS-T flight sensitivity consequently relied on younger-animal exposure support and only 20 age-restricted assignments. Unit leverage for the remaining older GC animal made some approximate HC3 gene uncertainties undefined; these were exported as unavailable rather than replaced with significant values. Leave-one-gene and leave-one-animal effects provide additional descriptive sensitivity outputs. The negative corrected tests limit regional claims but do not establish equivalence or the absence of biologically meaningful flight effects.')
P('A conservative cross-cohort gene screen required matching directions and support in every constituent regional contrast. Cerebellum and hippocampus each used RRRM-2 ISS-T, RRRM-2 LAR and RR-10 exposure contrasts; the left hemisphere used RRRM-2 and RR-10. Intersection–union probabilities were the maximum component Wald p, with discordant or unavailable effects assigned p=1. Across 54,989 region–gene combinations, no gene survived global BH or BY correction (Supplementary Fig. 1). Direction concordance alone was frequent: 5,047 cerebellar, 5,001 hippocampal and 9,884 left-hemisphere genes. Nominal max-p<0.05 counts were three, zero and 554, respectively. This distinction prevents the ranked candidate list from becoming a claim of newly validated flight-response genes.')
H('Score removal is an oblique projection with potentially large gain',2)
P('Let Y be a gene-by-sample normalized expression matrix and Y₀ its residual after nuisance-design adjustment. A unit ground-contrast direction v defines the genome-wide projection. A unit sensor-weight vector w is obtained by restricting the ground contrast to Sensor31; s=wᵀY₀ is its ground score, and γ is the genome-wide regression loading. The two transforms are:')
E('Aₚ = I − vvᵀ;     s = wᵀY₀;     γ = Y₀sᵀ/(ssᵀ);     Aₛ = I − γwᵀ.    (1)')
P('Because wᵀγ=1, the score operator is idempotent and removes the weighted sensor score. It is generally oblique rather than orthogonal. Writing γ=w+h with h perpendicular to w gives, for at least two genes:')
E('Aₛ² = Aₛ;     wᵀAₛ = 0;     ‖Aₛ‖₂ = ‖γ‖₂ = √(1 + ‖h‖₂²).    (2)')
P('A score can therefore vanish while other expression directions are amplified. The learned spectral gains ranged from 8.129 to 15.309 across all 15 aligned anchor–target geometries (Fig. 7a). The norm is a worst-direction amplification limit, not the observed gain of every gene or contrast. It depends on the retained gene universe and expression metric. Conditional OLS t statistics must be refitted after transforming expression; projecting an existing t-statistic vector does not generally give the transformed t statistics.')
P('To constrain this amplification, we fixed a gain cap L=2 before the bounded sensitivity analysis and shrank only the perpendicular component of the loading:')
E('c = min{1, √(L²−1)/‖h‖₂};     γᴸ = w + ch;     Aᴸ = I − γᴸwᵀ.    (3)')
P('When h=0, c is defined as one. The loading γᴸ is the unique closest vector to γ under wᵀγᴸ=1 and ‖γᴸ‖₂≤L. Hence the operator retains score removal and idempotence while limiting spectral gain to L. The result follows by projecting h onto a Euclidean ball in the hyperplane perpendicular to w (Supplementary Methods). For any positive-semidefinite covariance Σ and biological vector b:')
E('λmax(AᴸΣAᴸᵀ) ≤ L²λmax(Σ);     ‖Aᴸb−b‖₂ ≤ L|wᵀb|.    (4)')
P('At L=2 this permits up to fourfold directional variance, so it is not a variance-contraction theorem. Every sensor-orthogonal biological vector is preserved exactly, including effects supported entirely outside the sensors. A biological effect with a nonzero weighted sensor score is altered. Sensor-score cancellation is also distinct from eliminating each sensor gene or reducing aggregate sensor effect energy. Dense independent numerical tests checked these identities and bounds in 100 geometries, with maximum error about 1.1×10⁻¹⁴.')
H('Collection-score discrimination does not guarantee loading transfer',2)
P('To test real-data generalization with independent preprocessing, we exhaustively held out one of three animals from each age-by-ground-collection cell in the core regions (81 splits per region; eight training and four test animals). RR-23 used 200 prespecified distinct splits with six HGC plus five VGC in training and three of each in testing. Every split refiltered genes and estimated its geometric-mean normalization reference using training counts only. Held-out size factors used that reference. The 362 splits overlap, and the two regions share many source animals; together they use 30 unique ground animals rather than 362 independent validation units (Fig. 10).')
