H('Known-truth experiments separate preservation from recovery',2)
P('The primary Gaussian benchmark used five fully retained scenarios with 200 independent datasets each, 1,200 genes, 24 ground-training samples and 24 distinct target samples. Seven methods produced 7,000 rows, including partial-score and oracle-ground references; four operational methods are shown in Fig. 6. The oracle-ground reference knows the training nuisance direction and is not a target oracle under changed coupling. All parameters and repetitions were saved. Biological false-discovery proportion (FDP) compares conditional OLS/BH discoveries with the original biological coefficient vector; residual nuisance can create a true transformed mean difference at a biologically null gene. The metric is therefore not a proof of conditional statistical FDR failure or universal biological FDR control.')
P(f'''In the nuisance-only scenario, mean biological effect RMSE was {bv('confounded_no_biology','raw','biological_effect_RMSE'):.3f} for raw effects, {bv('confounded_no_biology','projection','biological_effect_RMSE'):.3f} for projection, {bv('confounded_no_biology','score','biological_effect_RMSE'):.3f} for score and {bv('confounded_no_biology','bounded_score','biological_effect_RMSE'):.3f} for bounded score. Biological FDP remained {bv('confounded_no_biology','projection','biological_false_discovery_proportion'):.3f}, {bv('confounded_no_biology','score','biological_false_discovery_proportion'):.3f} and {bv('confounded_no_biology','bounded_score','biological_false_discovery_proportion'):.3f} after the three corrections. With sensor-orthogonal biological effects, power was low: {bv('orthogonal_biology','projection','power'):.3f}, {bv('orthogonal_biology','score','power'):.3f} and {bv('orthogonal_biology','bounded_score','power'):.3f}. The correction does not turn a small noisy experiment into a high-power discovery study.''')
P(f'''When biology overlapped nuisance, score RMSE increased to {bv('overlapping_biology','score','biological_effect_RMSE'):.3f} versus raw {bv('overlapping_biology','raw','biological_effect_RMSE'):.3f}. Under changed target coupling, score and bounded-score RMSE were {bv('changed_coupling','score','biological_effect_RMSE'):.3f} and {bv('changed_coupling','bounded_score','biological_effect_RMSE'):.3f}, compared with raw {bv('changed_coupling','raw','biological_effect_RMSE'):.3f}; biological FDP rose to {bv('changed_coupling','score','biological_false_discovery_proportion'):.3f} and {bv('changed_coupling','bounded_score','biological_false_discovery_proportion'):.3f}. These failures are integral results. Energy retention for a known biological vector is not sufficient to establish accurate recovery of the observed confounded contrast.''')
P('The primary benchmark\'s learned gain was about 2.14, below the real-data range. An explicitly exploratory amendment therefore tested 16 combinations of true gain 8 or 12, training size 24 or 96, target size 24 or 48, and unchanged or reversed outside-sensor coupling, with 100 independent repetitions each (Fig. 9). Learned gains ranged 6.712–12.573; bounded gain remained at most two within floating-point tolerance. Both score operators preserved the known sensor-orthogonal biological vector exactly. Under unchanged coupling, equally weighted descriptive RMSE across settings was 1.648 raw, 0.388 projection, 0.401 score and 1.377 bounded score. Under reversed coupling the corresponding values were 1.633, 0.502, 3.093 and 1.889. Bounding moderated the original score\'s amplification under reversal, but substantial biological FDP remained and nuisance removal deteriorated when true nuisance required a gain far above two.')
P('An explanatory fixed-sensor isotropic-noise calculation further shows how high dimensionality can inflate an estimated loading even when its population gain is one. With residual sample rank r>2 and G genes, the expected squared loading norm is one plus (G−1)/(r−2). An independent 1,000-repetition check with G=1,200 and r=23 gave mean squared gain 59.141 versus the theoretical 58.095. This special case does not give the exact sampling law of the present adaptive, correlated estimator. It identifies a reason to report gain and training sample size, rather than treating a fitted loading as known.')
H('Discussion')
P('The strongest conclusion is an operational distinction between collection-cohort association, correction stability and biological recovery. Ground animals exhibit a rapid-response cohort gradient, showing that a relevant expression pattern can exist without flight. The same pattern may also carry genuine exposure-dependent biology. A correction learned from it can remove a weighted score algebraically, yet amplify other directions or discard overlapping biology. The real datasets and independent simulations expose each link in this argument; no step permits the removed energy to be interpreted as the fraction of artifacts eliminated.')
P('The bounded construction offers a transparent safeguard with a precise limit. Its closest-loading interpretation, score-null preservation and covariance bound are useful because they are verifiable independently of the desired results. They do not select the scientifically correct nuisance direction. The high-gain amendment demonstrates the price of that guarantee: when nuisance is diffuse and genuinely requires large oblique gain, an aggressive cap leaves it behind. Under changed coupling the cap reduces the original score\'s instability but still fails to restore reliable biology. Thus the bounded result should be reported alongside raw and orthogonal-projection effects, not promoted as a universally superior replacement for established normalization or unwanted-variation methods [11,12,19].')
P('The held-out-ground experiment adds a concrete real-data counterexample to interpreting sensor success as global correction success. Training-only references and sensors classified the core ground cohorts correctly in every balanced holdout, yet genome-wide loading prediction worsened on average in two of three settings. Thus this failure is not solely an artificial Gaussian domain-shift construction. The high-dimensional loading can be unreliable even when its selected score is highly repeatable. The available 30 ground animals and overlapping splits support a within-resource robustness result; they do not establish performance in a new mission or randomized collection experiment.')
P('A practical analysis can predeclare the sensor set and cap, report the gene universe and learned gain, quantify ground-animal selection sensitivity, and retain negative transfer results. It should refit statistics after each expression transformation and propagate estimated normalization and anchor uncertainty. A ground-only diagnostic can detect numerical instability, but cannot determine whether the shared direction represents collection, biological exposure, or both. Held-out ground samples help assess stability within a collection process; they do not establish transfer to a different flight, recovery or tissue context. The joint bootstrap improves uncertainty accounting while remaining conditional on the observed strata and fixed retained genes.')
P('The biological evidence deserves equally careful treatment. The hippocampal SRG association is supported in the full ISS-T comparison but loses corrected support under exclusions that materially change age representation. The paired tests do not demonstrate regional specificity. The cross-cohort screen identifies no corrected reproducible gene, despite many same-direction estimates. These outcomes do not invalidate prior evidence of neuronal or vascular responses [15–17], whose assays and experimental contexts differ. They delimit what this processed bulk resource can establish. An absence of corrected discoveries is especially compatible with small sample sizes, heterogeneous exposure and incomplete harmonization; it is not a demonstration of biological equivalence.')
P('The direct Circadian interaction adds a focused, testable biological candidate: the flight–ground difference depends on collection cohort in the full hippocampal data. Its direction survives omission checks and count-model estimation, but exclusion sensitivity loses corrected support as age-cell representation collapses. Circadian output and erythroid-associated interactions should therefore guide matched-collection and cell-composition follow-up, without being promoted as a new causal clock or universal neuronal response. The strong beta-globin associations may reflect composition or collection-linked biology; the present counts cannot distinguish these explanations.')
P('Interpretation also depends on the panel definitions. They were inherited before this extension, overlap and contain genes with multiple functions. Rapid, delayed and secondary labels are mechanistic hypotheses informed by prior response studies [6,7], not validated temporal bins in these mice. Missing genes are reported rather than substituted by favorable aliases. Glial and endothelial panels summarize bulk transcript abundance, which can change through tissue composition, within-cell expression or normalization. Claims about inflammasome activation, specific glial states or barrier permeability require orthogonal cell-resolved, protein or functional measurements. This manuscript does not use those panel names as assay substitutes.')
P('Several limitations remain material. The study uses deposited processed counts and does not re-align raw reads or re-assess every library\'s sequence-level quality. Sex and strain differ among cohorts. ISS-T versus LAR combines exposure duration and recovery; RR-23 ground comparisons differ in handling and harvest date. Location metadata conflicts are unresolved. Label-permutation inference depends on exchangeability rather than randomized collection assignment, while CR1 and HC3 gene-level approximations can be fragile with few animals or high leverage. Conditional probabilities after estimated transformations are not calibrated discoveries about unobserved biological effects. The Gaussian benchmarks isolate mechanisms but do not simulate every count, dispersion, zero or cell-composition feature of RNA-seq. The high-gain configurations were added after inspecting the primary benchmark and are labelled exploratory.')
P('Future experimental validation should cross exposure with matched collection and preservation protocols, include sufficient independent animals at both ages, and retain paired anatomical sampling. A collection time course could evaluate the proposed response panels directly; cell-resolved and functional assays could then test whether the hippocampal candidates reflect neuronal regulation or composition. Algorithms should be challenged with negative controls justified independently of the exposure, and with nuisance–biology overlap and coupling changes specified before testing. Such designs address the missing identifiability more directly than adding post hoc significant gene sets to the same observational matrices.')
P('Taken together, these analyses provide a reproducible framework for auditing correction in spaceflight brain transcriptomics. The defensible advance is a measured diagnosis of transfer failure and a proven amplification constraint, accompanied by paired and cross-cohort limits on biological claims. Stronger causal conclusions require collection-controlled data; the available evidence already supports reporting when a correction fails, rather than treating numerical attenuation as a successful recovery of biology.')
H('Methods')
H('Data, identity and preprocessing',2)
P('Public processed STAR count matrices and deposited metadata were assembled for OSD-525, OSD-561, OSD-562, OSD-563, OSD-564, OSD-612 and OSD-613 [23–29]. A matched RSEM matrix was used for OSD-525 estimator sensitivity. No new animals or laboratory experiments were used. Checksums, source URLs, retrieved metadata snapshots and the independent core STAR verification are supplied. Source Name was the primary animal identifier and was crosschecked against RFID. Technical libraries within animal and deposit were summed before filtering or inference. Cross-region pairing required exact source identity, and all regional records of a metadata-conflict animal were removed together in the exclusion sensitivity.')
P('Within each deposit, canonical ENSMUSG identifiers with mean raw count≥10 were retained. Median-ratio factors used genes positive in every sample: each sample\'s median log ratio to the across-sample geometric mean was exponentiated. Expression was log₂(count/size factor+1). Gene symbols were mapped using the bundled Ensembl release 112 annotation; aliases were not silently substituted. Deposits were normalized separately, and pooled analyses aligned intersecting genes while including tissue and age nuisance terms. The initial gene universe remained fixed for the joint bootstrap. These preprocessing choices make effect sizes relative normalized bulk-expression measures.')
H('Contrast inference and multiplicity',2)
P('Linear contrasts used an intercept and available age terms; pooled core contrasts also adjusted for tissue. Singular-value decomposition identified the nuisance rank. Frisch–Waugh–Lovell residualization yielded the exposure/cohort coefficient. Gene uncertainties used animal-cluster CR1 sandwich estimates and t probabilities with cluster-count-minus-one degrees of freedom, exported as exploratory. Panel effects were mean constituent gene coefficients. Exact label permutations were restricted by the stated age blocks, preserved positive group counts and kept repeated regions of each animal together. The empirical probability included the observed assignment; the Monte Carlo plus-one alternative exists in code but was not needed for the current primary panel contrasts [20].')
P('Primary panel intervals used 1,000 animal bootstraps within group and age, retaining all regional records of each selected animal and holding normalized expression fixed. All 232 panel probabilities across primary contrasts and sensitivities were corrected together using BH [10]. The rapid-minus-secondary gradient formed a separate 29-test family. BH relies on appropriate dependence conditions; the overlapping panels and observational exchangeability assumptions limit universal guarantees. Ground-anchor conditional panel probabilities formed a separate sensitivity family and are not reported as corrected biological discoveries. Simulation and gene-screen families are stated separately below.')
H('Negative-binomial robustness and regional reproducibility',2)
P('PyDESeq2 0.5.4 fitted seven per-deposit count models [8,9]. The two core regional models used age plus a four-level flight-by-cohort condition; OSD-613 used age plus exposure, OSD-525 used three exposure/control groups, and RR-10 used exposure. Models used integer collapsed counts, parametric dispersion trends and Cook-refitting/filtering, with independent filtering disabled and no coefficient shrinkage. Fifteen regional contrast outputs were compared with corresponding linear panel means. Counts were not pooled across anatomical deposits in these models. Direction agreement and coefficient correlation are descriptive estimator checks; panel means of gene Wald coefficients do not constitute a new inferential panel test.')
P('The reproducibility screen aligned genes across each region\'s listed exposure contrasts and used the maximum finite two-sided Wald p value as the intersection–union probability. Nonconcordant signs or unavailable effects received p=1. Component independence is not required by the maximum-p intersection–union construction, provided component probabilities are valid. All 54,989 tests formed one global BH family; BY sensitivity multiplied BH q values by the family harmonic sum for arbitrary dependence. Shared fitted dispersion and age parameters do not provide independent biological replication. Ranked candidates remain exploratory even when individual component probabilities are small.')
H('Paired regional analysis',2)
P('For 24 RRRM-2 and ten RR-10 matched animals, each panel\'s hippocampal mean log-expression minus cerebellar mean was the animal-level outcome. Regional exposure contrasts used age-restricted complete label enumeration; pooled RRRM-2 flight labels additionally respected collection cohort. Regional cohort contrasts were tested within flight or ground groups. The approximate three-way coefficient used additive age and flight-by-cohort terms, with HC3 studentization and 9,999 restricted-model Rademacher wild draws. All 104 panel tests, including metadata sensitivities, formed one BH family. Percentile intervals used 2,000 resamples within exposure, cohort and age. Gene-level HC3 outputs were flagged unavailable at unit leverage. The paired program separately verified its sandwich estimate, exact permutation probabilities and family correction.')
H('Within-region flight by collection interaction',2)
P('This extension used all core animals and the conflict-animal exclusions in each region. The full design was intercept, age29, flight, ISS-T and flight×ISS-T. The numeric contrast (0,0,0,0,1) estimates (FLT−GC in ISS-T)−(FLT−GC in LAR). Eight inherited panels in two regions and two sensitivities formed one additional exploratory 32-test BH family. Intervals used 4,000 stratified whole-animal bootstraps; approximate probabilities used 499,999 restricted-model Rademacher wild draws with HC3 studentization. Monte Carlo exceedance intervals, all animal/gene omission effects and age-cell support are supplied. Four PyDESeq2 fits used the identical five-column design and unshrunk numeric interaction contrast. The 72,023 evaluable gene probabilities were corrected together; Cook-filtered unavailable probabilities remained unavailable. An independent four-group parameterization verified the difference-in-differences, sandwich and Wald calculations; unstable non-significant gene-fit discrepancies are disclosed. Seven fixed erythroid markers were assessed descriptively using the existing gene family, without a new favorable gene-set significance test. Residual marker correlations removed the entire design and omitted each target transcript from its marker score.')
H('Ground anchors, transfer and resampling',2)
P('Core ground anchors used GC ISS-T versus GC LAR, adjusted for age. RR-23 used HGC versus VGC. The sensor consisted of the rPRG-plus-HSP union, with available genes reported per aligned universe. The ground coefficient defined v and the restricted unit weight w; residual ground scores defined γ as in Eq. 1. Operators were applied to normalized expression, after gene alignment, before refitting the target contrast. All 15 applications retained raw, orthogonal-projection, original-score and bounded-score outputs. The gain cap L=2 was a fixed diagnostic choice, not tuned to significance or biological accuracy. Its construction and limitations are proved in Supplementary Methods.')
P('For sensor or outside-sensor set S, energy removal compared the squared raw coefficient β with the refitted transformed coefficient β*. Negative reductions mean amplification and were retained:')
E('Energy removal(S) = 100[1 − Σg∈S(β*g)² / Σg∈Sβg²].    (5)')
P('Ground-only uncertainty used 200 stratified animal bootstraps with a fixed target and fixed normalization. Three matched applications additionally used 500 joint stratified bootstraps, relearning normalization, anchor and target, with shared OSD-525 HGC selected once for both roles. RR-23 partitions exhaustively chose four of nine HGC for training, used the remaining five for evaluation and included all eight VGC in every anchor. Shared normalization before partitioning and reuse across partitions are explicitly disclosed. λ was median χ²₁-equivalent conditional OLS probability divided by the χ²₁ median; it is a dispersion diagnostic.')
H('Ground-only prediction validation',2)
P('Ground holdouts used training-only canonical-gene mean-count filtering and normalization references. Target factors were median log ratios to positive training-gene geometric means, using nonzero target entries. Ground score orientation was fixed by the positive training cohort. Age-stratified AUC counted held-out positive–negative pair orderings within age. Outside-sensor prediction compared the loading times the held-out sensor residual score with held-out expression residualized using training-fitted intercept and age. The baseline used those nuisance means alone. Nested ground-training leave-one-out selection used the five fixed strengths and fully refitted filtering, normalization, nuisance and sensor estimation. No flight data or outer held-out errors selected the shrinkage. Reported split quantiles are overlapping-split robustness ranges, not sample-independent confidence limits. Verification reconstructed six first/last cases directly from source counts and checked all allocations and training-CV minimizers.')
H('Simulation design and mathematical verification',2)
P('The primary benchmark generated five scenarios with 200 independent repetitions each, following explicit aims, data-generating mechanisms, estimands, methods and performance measures [13]. Each dataset contained 1,200 genes, 31 sensors, 151 nuisance-support genes, 24 ground and 24 independent target samples, heteroskedastic Gaussian gene noise and five signed correlated factors. Sixty sensor-disjoint biological effects had magnitude 0.8; the overlapping scenario instead included nuisance-aligned biology. Target nuisance coupling was either maintained or altered. No method was selected using target biological truth. The seven retained methods were raw, genome projection, score, bounded score, half-score, oracle-ground projection and oracle-ground score.')
P('Metrics included coefficient RMSE against the true biological vector, power, biological FDP, known-vector energy retention and spectral gain. Gene calls used conditional OLS probabilities with BH within each simulated method/dataset. FDP was zero when no calls occurred; power and retention were unavailable when no biological effect existed. Summary files provide repetition dispersion and Monte Carlo standard errors; figure uncertainty is ±1.96 standard errors of the mean. The high-gain amendment used all 16 combinations of gain, training size, target size and coupling reversal, with 100 independent repetitions each. Its exact configuration and results were retained, including failures. Independent dense-matrix numerical tests checked production transformations against SVD, covariance and constrained-loading calculations; the fixed-sensor noise calculation had a separate 1,000-repetition verification.')
H('Reporting, software and AI assistance',2)
P('All analyses were exploratory reanalyses, with no prospective animal sample-size calculation, randomized allocation or investigator blinding. No post hoc power calculation was used to declare absence of effects. Counts, full contrast manifests, unavailable-value flags, tested families and algorithm failures are supplied rather than selected discovery-only tables. Scientific software used Python 3.12.13, NumPy 2.5.3, pandas 3.0.5, SciPy 1.18.1 and PyDESeq2 0.5.4; reproducible seeds and complete configurations are recorded. Substantial AI assistance through OpenAI Codex was used to audit metadata and code, develop mathematical and computational analyses, retrieve source records, draft text and prepare figures. Authors are responsible for reviewing scientific interpretation, code and final wording; AI systems are not authors. Source citations were checked against DOI metadata and, for key interpretations, primary full text.')
H('Data availability')
P('Source data are publicly accessible through NASA OSDR under OSD-525, OSD-561, OSD-562, OSD-563, OSD-564, OSD-612 and OSD-613 [23–29]. The accompanying review package contains the exact processed inputs used here, retrieved metadata snapshots, checksums, full derived tables and figure sources. No controlled-access human data were used. A permanent public archive for this revision has not yet been created; its identifier must be inserted before publication. The analysis does not claim that the original data were generated by the manuscript authors.')
H('Code availability')
P('Executable Python scripts, dependency specifications, tests, full simulation configurations and a file-verification manifest accompany the manuscript. The README states the complete reproduction order and distinguishes cached count-model reuse from forced refitting. A versioned public code release and permanent archive identifier remain a submission preparation step; no nonexistent repository or DOI is supplied.')
