H('Supplementary Methods',blocks=supp)
H('S1. Projection geometry and its scope',2,blocks=supp)
P('Let G≥2 and w be unit length. The loading estimate satisfies wᵀγ=1 because s=wᵀY₀ and γ=Y₀sᵀ/(ssᵀ). The denominator must be positive. With A=I−γwᵀ, A²=I−2γwᵀ+γ(wᵀγ)wᵀ=A. Its range is w-perpendicular and kernel is the span of γ. This projection is orthogonal only for γ=w. Write h=γ−w, so wᵀh=0. In the orthonormal basis w,h/‖h‖, its nontrivial block has rows (0,0) and (−‖h‖,1). Its nonzero block singular value is √(1+‖h‖²); G−2 additional singular values are one, and the last is zero. For G=1, the operator is zero, so the stated norm equality needs the dimension qualification.',blocks=supp)
P('A sensor-score identity does not imply gene-wise removal. For w=(1,0,0)ᵀ, γ=(1,2,3)ᵀ and β=(1,0,0)ᵀ, Aβ=(0,−2,−3)ᵀ. The first coordinate score is removed while the two-coordinate sensor-set energy can increase. If w is supported on sensor set S and O is its nonempty complement, ‖PᴼA‖₂=√(1+‖γᴼ‖²). Outside-sensor amplification is possible even when wᵀAY=0. A genome-wide orthogonal projection has norm one, but it can still remove a biological component aligned with its fitted direction.',blocks=supp)
H('S2. Closest-loading gain constraint',2,blocks=supp)
P('For L≥1, constrain g by wᵀg=1 and ‖g‖≤L. Every admissible g has form w+k with k perpendicular to w and ‖k‖≤√(L²−1). Minimizing ‖g−γ‖² reduces to projecting h onto this closed Euclidean ball. Strict convexity gives the unique solution k=c h, with c=min(1,√(L²−1)/‖h‖), using c=1 if h=0. Therefore the bounded loading retains the sensor identity, idempotence and closest-loading interpretation, with spectral gain at most L. At L=1 it becomes I−wwᵀ and leaves all outside-sensor coordinates unchanged. This standard constrained geometry is applied as a sensitivity safeguard; L=2 has not been estimated as biologically optimal.',blocks=supp)
P('For positive-semidefinite Σ, any unit x satisfies xᵀAᴸΣAᴸᵀx≤λmax(Σ)‖Aᴸᵀx‖²≤L²λmax(Σ). For any biological vector b, Aᴸb−b=−γᴸ(wᵀb), giving the norm bound in Eq. 4. Every b with wᵀb=0 is preserved exactly, but its recovery from observed data also depends on remaining nuisance and sampling noise. Energy may increase despite distorted direction; neither energy retention nor score cancellation is a truth criterion.',blocks=supp)
E('If β = b + δu, then Aᴸβ − b = −γᴸ(wᵀb) + δ[u − γᴸ(wᵀu)].    (S1)',blocks=supp)
P('The first term is biological distortion; the second is residual nuisance. Ground associations alone cannot identify their separation. If u=(wᵀu)γ is exactly transferable nuisance, the bounded operator leaves Aᴸu=(wᵀu)(1−c)h. Thus reducing gain can deliberately sacrifice nuisance removal even under perfect transfer. The observed high-gain unchanged-coupling failures are consistent with this identity.',blocks=supp)
H('S3. High-dimensional loading uncertainty under fixed sensors',2,blocks=supp)
P('For an explanatory special case, assume fixed w selected independently of data, isotropic independent Gaussian residual expression and residual sample rank r. Rotate gene space to w=e₁. Then score squared length follows χ²ᵣ and, conditional on the score, each of G−1 perpendicular loading coordinates has variance 1/(sᵀs). The perpendicular squared loading is the ratio of independent chi-squared variables with G−1 and r degrees of freedom. Its expectation and variance are:',blocks=supp)
E('E‖γ̂‖² = 1 + (G−1)/(r−2),  r>2;    (S2)',blocks=supp)
E('Var(‖γ̂‖²) = 2(G−1)(G+r−3)/[(r−2)²(r−4)],  r>4.    (S3)',blocks=supp)
P('The 1,000-repetition independent check used G=1,200, N=24 and an intercept (r=23). The theoretical mean was 58.0952 and observed mean 59.1409, within 1.77 theoretical Monte Carlo standard errors. This explains a possible finite-sample high-dimensional gain mechanism but does not specify the law of the present adaptive w, correlated expression or heteroskedastic ground estimator. The saved script and test result identify every assumption.',blocks=supp)
H('S4. Complete simulation configurations and performance definitions',2,blocks=supp)
config=json.loads((R/'known_truth_benchmark_config.json').read_text())
T('Supplementary Table S1. Primary mechanism benchmark configuration.',['Parameter','Value'],[[k,json.dumps(v) if isinstance(v,(dict,list)) else str(v)]for k,v in config.items() if k not in ['oracle_description']],blocks=supp)
P('The oracle label in the original configuration means the true ground nuisance, including under changed target coupling. It is an upper-information reference about the ground mechanism and is not uniformly best or a target truth oracle. For independent target Gaussian errors with a correct sample design and a fixed independently trained operator, marginal conditional OLS gene tests can remain valid despite induced cross-gene dependence. Biological false calls use the original b as truth; residual nuisance can therefore make the transformed conditional null false at a biological-null gene. Empirical biological FDP cannot be interpreted as a theorem of biological FDR control. General signed dependence also does not guarantee classical BH validity under all configurations.',blocks=supp)
P('Each performance mean is computed over the full retained repetitions. RMSE is the square root of mean squared error between the estimated coefficient and the true biological effect; outside-sensor RMSE restricts the calculation to the complement. Power is the called fraction of nonzero b entries; biological FDP is the called fraction with b=0, assigned zero when no genes are called. Known-vector energy retention is ‖Ab‖²/‖b‖² and is unavailable when b=0. Summary lower/upper95 columns in the primary simulation file are empirical repetition percentiles, not confidence limits for the mean; figure bars instead use 1.96 times the explicitly supplied Monte Carlo standard error. Full oracle and half-score results remain in the tables.',blocks=supp)
high=json.loads((HERE/'extensions/mathematics_review/high_gain_benchmark_config.json').read_text())
